h tables involved in the join but can be logically applied to either input of the join to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs.\n- In this query, Case 1 is matched because the filter conditions `ct.kind = 'production companies'` and `it.info = 'bottom 10 rank'` do not reference columns from both tables involved in the joins. These conditions can be logically applied to either input of the join to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs.\n\nStep 4: Based on the matched case, we can explain the SQL query changes as follows:\n\nThe query rewrite rule applies Case 1 to the given SQL query. This involves moving filter conditions that do not reference columns from both tables involved in the joins into the ON clause of the respective INNER JOIN operations. In this query, the filter conditions `ct.kind = 'production companies'` and `it.info = 'bottom 10 rank'` are moved from the WHERE clause to the ON clause of the first two join operations, respectively. This reduces the size of datasets before the joins occur, improving query performance.", 'time': 9.661773681640625}
